(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-most-likely-45-version-3"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 2: Most Likely 45 (Version 3: Drug Names)","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 62-year-old is newly diagnosed with open-angle glaucoma and has risk factors for progression. Which drug is the usual first choice?","choices":["Brimonidine","Latanoprost","Dorzolamide","Pilocarpine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Latanoprost, a prostaglandin analog (with travoprost, bimatoprost and tafluprost), is the first-line glaucoma class and the most commonly used; it lowers pressure by increasing aqueous outflow. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 64 and 65"},{"q":"A 19-year-old who wears contact lenses has purulent bacterial conjunctivitis, and keratitis has been excluded. Which ophthalmic antibiotic is the preferred treatment?","choices":["Erythromycin","Ciprofloxacin","Tobramycin","Sulfacetamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ciprofloxacin, a fluoroquinolone, covers Pseudomonas aeruginosa, the gram-negative rod that contact lens wearers are at risk from; fluoroquinolones are also preferred for corneal ulcers. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 21 and 22"},{"q":"Which glaucoma agent is BETA 1 SELECTIVE?","choices":["Betaxolol","Timolol","Carteolol","Levobunolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The selective one, so it carries less risk of bronchoconstriction in a patient with asthma. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 47-year-old farmer develops fungal keratitis after a branch scratches his cornea while he clears brush. Which drug is the one commercially available ophthalmic antifungal?","choices":["Natamycin","Trifluridine","Ciprofloxacin","Erythromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Natamycin is the only commercially available ophthalmic antifungal; it binds sterol in the fungal membrane, makes it leak, and covers Aspergillus, Candida, Fusarium and others. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 30 and 32"},{"q":"A 60-year-old with a strong family history of glaucoma needs a topical steroid for anterior uveitis. Which drug is a soft steroid with a lower risk of raising intraocular pressure?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Prednisolone","Difluprednate","Loteprednol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loteprednol is one of the three soft steroids (with fluorometholone and rimexolone), marked because they carry a lower risk of raising eye pressure, which matters in a patient at risk of glaucoma. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which glaucoma agent is CONTRAINDICATED in children under two years of age?","choices":["Latanoprost","Timolol","Brimonidine","Dorzolamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The alpha-2 agonists, because of central nervous system depression and apnea. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which ocular anesthetic leaves the eye numb for 10 to 20 minutes with NO BLINK REFLEX?","choices":["Tropicamide","Proparacaine","Phenylephrine","Fluorescein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. True of both ocular anesthetics, and the reason you must not write prescriptions for them. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A patient with a documented sulfonamide allergy has bacterial conjunctivitis. Which ophthalmic antibiotic must be avoided?","choices":["Bacitracin","Tobramycin","Sulfacetamide","Trimethoprim with polymyxin B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is a sulfonamide, so a sulfa allergy rules it out. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which eye drop causes rebound hyperemia if it is used for too long?","choices":["Olopatadine","Cromolyn","Ketorolac","Naphazoline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Naphazoline is a topical vasoconstrictor, and prolonged use leads to rebound hyperemia after discontinuation; nasal decongestant sprays share the risk. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A bus driver with seasonal allergies must stay alert on a 10-hour route. Which antihistamine should he take?","choices":["Diphenhydramine","Hydroxyzine","Fexofenadine","Promethazine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fexofenadine is a second-generation antihistamine; it largely stays out of the central nervous system and is rated very low for sedation, so it treats the allergy without making a driver drowsy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slides 32 and 37"},{"q":"Which cough drug is contraindicated within 2 weeks of taking a MONOAMINE OXIDASE INHIBITOR?","choices":["Benzonatate","Guaifenesin","Dextromethorphan","N-acetylcysteine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dextromethorphan raises the risk of serotonin syndrome, so it is contraindicated during or within 2 weeks of a monoamine oxidase inhibitor. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 40-year-old who uses an inhaled steroid develops white patches in the mouth and takes several other medicines. Which antifungal is preferred?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Natamycin","Amphotericin B","Nystatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nystatin is a nonabsorbable antifungal that treats oral candidiasis (thrush), which appears with inhaled steroids; because it is not absorbed it cannot interact with the patient's other medicines. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 4-year-old with acute otitis media is allergic to penicillin. Which third-generation cephalosporin is the oral choice?","choices":["Cefdinir","Cephalexin","Ceftriaxone","Azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cefdinir is the third-generation oral cephalosporin named for penicillin allergy and for failed therapy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 7-year-old with chickenpox has a fever. Which drug should be avoided because it is linked to Reye syndrome?","choices":["Aspirin","Ibuprofen","Acetaminophen","Naproxen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin in children with fever from a viral illness raises the incidence of Reye syndrome. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which antifungal is a CYTOCHROME P450 3A4 INHIBITOR that prolongs the corrected QT interval?","choices":["Nystatin","Natamycin","Ketoconazole","Amphotericin B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ketoconazole inhibits cytochrome P450 3A4 and prolongs the corrected QT interval (QTc). — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which ear drop is NOT recommended with a RUPTURED EARDRUM or tubes in place, because of cochlear damage and hearing loss?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin/dexamethasone","Ofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Neomycin/polymyxin B/hydrocortisone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The polymyxin B in these Cortisporin drops can reach the inner ear through a ruptured eardrum or tubes, causing cochlear damage and hearing loss. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which nasal spray causes REBOUND RHINITIS (rhinitis medicamentosa) if used for more than a few days?","choices":["Azelastine","Oxymetazoline","Mometasone","Pseudoephedrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oxymetazoline (Afrin) causes rebound rhinitis (rhinitis medicamentosa) if used for more than 3 to 5 days. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which ORAL second-generation antihistamine is rated LOW, rather than very low, for sedation?","choices":["Fexofenadine","Cetirizine","Loratadine","Chlorpheniramine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cetirizine is rated low for sedation, while the other oral second-generation agents, fexofenadine and loratadine, are rated very low. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 64-year-old with chronic kidney disease who uses a potassium-containing salt substitute needs a new antihypertensive. Which drug is most likely to raise his potassium?","choices":["Amlodipine","Prazosin","Lisinopril","Hydralazine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lisinopril, an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor, lowers aldosterone, so potassium rises, most often with renal disease, potassium-sparing diuretics, potassium supplements or salt substitutes. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient with hypertension also has heart failure. Which calcium channel blocker approved for hypertension avoids the relative contraindication in heart failure?","choices":["Verapamil","Amlodipine","Diltiazem","Nimodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is a relative contraindication to the non-dihydropyridines because they suppress contractility; amlodipine, a dihydropyridine approved for hypertension, has little effect on contractility. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 52-year-old with type 2 diabetes and newly diagnosed hypertension needs a first drug that also protects the kidneys. Which drug is preferred?","choices":["Atenolol","Prazosin","Hydralazine","Lisinopril"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lisinopril, an ACE inhibitor, is preferred in diabetes because it lowers glomerular pressure and slows diabetic nephropathy; an angiotensin receptor blocker is the equivalent choice. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 68-year-old with atrial fibrillation needs his heart rate slowed with a calcium channel blocker. Which drug is the right choice?","choices":["Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Felodipine","Diltiazem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem, a non-dihydropyridine, suppresses the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes, so atrial fibrillation or flutter is an approved use; only diltiazem and verapamil act on the heart this way. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A patient cannot tolerate an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor's cough. Which drug is the SWITCH?","choices":["Enalapril","Valsartan","Captopril","Ramipril"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not cause cough and have a lower incidence of angioedema, so patients may be switched to them. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which beta blocker ALSO BLOCKS ALPHA-1 and is used in heart failure?","choices":["Metoprolol","Atenolol","Carvedilol","Nadolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol is a third-generation agent with alpha-1 blockade, antioxidant and antiproliferative actions; it is a heart failure agent. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is one of the three used for HEART FAILURE?","choices":["Bisoprolol","Propranolol","Atenolol","Timolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The heart failure beta blockers are carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol, started very low and increased slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which drug is N-ACETYLATED, with LUPUS SYNDROME in slow acetylators?","choices":["Minoxidil","Nitroprusside","Prazosin","Hydralazine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine lupus syndrome risk: high dose, long term, women, slow acetylators and Caucasian ancestry. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Which drug given by INFUSION for HYPERTENSIVE CRISIS can cause CYANIDE toxicity?","choices":["Nitroprusside","Hydralazine","Minoxidil","Nicardipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nitroprusside carries five cyanide groups; sodium thiosulfate limits cyanide toxicity, and thiocyanate builds up with long infusions or renal failure. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man after a myocardial infarction needs lipid-lowering therapy. Which drug is first line?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Cholestyramine","Atorvastatin","Niacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin is a statin, the first-line drug when LDL (low-density lipoprotein) lowering is indicated; clinical atherosclerotic disease such as a prior infarction is the first statin benefit group. — Lipids.pptx, Slides 25 and 65"},{"q":"A patient with high triglycerides has existing gallbladder disease. Which agent should be avoided?","choices":["Niacin","Fenofibrate","Atorvastatin","Evolocumab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Existing gallbladder disease contraindicates fibrates, which cause cholelithiasis. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A man's lipid panel shows normal cholesterol but a triglyceride level of 1200 mg/dL. Which drug is the best choice for this problem?","choices":["Colesevelam","Ezetimibe","Evolocumab","Fenofibrate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fenofibrate is a fibrate; triglycerides above 1000 mg/dL are the primary fibrate indication, and fibrates lower triglycerides by 20 to 50 percent. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A patient with a fasting triglyceride of 600 mg/dL and a high LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol is about to start lipid therapy. Which drug is contraindicated?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Colestipol","Niacin","Atorvastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Colestipol, a bile acid sequestrant, is contraindicated when triglycerides exceed 400 mg/dL (relatively above 200 mg/dL) because resins can raise very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) production and triglycerides. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient on verapamil needs a statin. Which statin is least affected by the inhibition of cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4)?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Lovastatin","Simvastatin","Rosuvastatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rosuvastatin's major metabolic route is listed as minimal CYP, unlike the CYP3A4 statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which drug INCREASES the ANTICOAGULANT EFFECT of warfarin?","choices":["Gemfibrozil","Colesevelam","Ezetimibe","Evolocumab"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates increase the anticoagulant effect of warfarin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which drug causes PROSTAGLANDIN-MEDIATED FLUSHING, minimized by aspirin?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Ezetimibe","Colestipol","Niacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Niacin flushing is prostaglandin mediated; aspirin premedication minimizes it. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which drug is an injectable MONOCLONAL ANTIBODY against PCSK9?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Colesevelam","Alirocumab","Gemfibrozil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alirocumab and evolocumab are the injectable-only, expensive PCSK9 inhibitors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which lipid-lowering drug is APPROVED IN PREGNANCY because it is not absorbed?","choices":["Cholestyramine","Atorvastatin","Fenofibrate","Simvastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bile acid sequestrants are not absorbed, so they are approved for children, adolescents and pregnancy. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with stable angina still has exertional attacks on metoprolol alone. Which added drug is best?","choices":["Verapamil","Enalapril","Amlodipine","Clopidogrel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amlodipine, a dihydropyridine, is the calcium channel blocker added when a beta blocker alone fails; it lowers wall tension without slowing atrioventricular conduction, and the beta blocker covers the heart rate rise that dihydropyridines can cause. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slides 22 and 23"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man presents two hours after the onset of crushing chest pain. The electrocardiogram shows ST-segment elevation in the anterior leads. He has no bleeding history, no prior stroke and no recent surgery. Which drug restores coronary flow by dissolving the clot?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Alteplase","Clopidogrel","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A fibrinolytic activates plasminogen bound to fibrin, dissolving the clot in an early ST-elevation myocardial infarction with no contraindication. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with exertional angina stopped his beta blocker because of vivid nightmares. Which drug is the usual substitute?","choices":["Carvedilol","Isosorbide mononitrate","Diltiazem","Clopidogrel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem, a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, slows heart rate and contractility like a beta blocker, so it is the substitute when a beta blocker is not tolerated. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has recurrent night-time chest pain at rest with transient ST-segment elevation, diagnosed as variant angina. Which drug should be avoided?","choices":["Propranolol","Amlodipine","Nitroglycerin","Diltiazem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol, a beta blocker, may worsen variant angina because the problem is vessel spasm, not oxygen demand; calcium channel blockers and nitrates reduce the symptoms. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which drug must never be combined with nitrates because the result can be severe hypotension, myocardial infarction or stroke?","choices":["Metoprolol","Amlodipine","Diltiazem","Sildenafil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sildenafil, tadalafil and vardenafil inhibit phosphodiesterase type 5 and must not be used with nitrates. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which drug is recommended for patients who are allergic to aspirin?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Clopidogrel","Heparin","Alteplase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clopidogrel is as efficacious as aspirin in secondary prevention and is recommended when aspirin causes allergy. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which drug is added for chest pain that does not respond to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Morphine","Nitroglycerin","Aspirin","Metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Morphine, an opioid analgesic, manages chest pain unresponsive to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker should be avoided in its short-acting form?","choices":["Amlodipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting agents are to be avoided, and nifedipine is the example named. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker is the alternative to a beta blocker when left ventricular function is decreased?","choices":["Amlodipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With decreased left ventricular function, a beta blocker is first line and amlodipine is the calcium channel blocker alternative. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
